data item_wholesale_supply_channel: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "wholesale-supply-channel",
  workstream: AcquisitionMarketing,
  milestone: M3YearEndGate,
  rank: 60,
  min_calendar_days: 14,
  active_effort_days: 14,
  execution_mode: ExternalLead,
  exit_criterion: "THE BINDING OFFER (recut 2026-09-14; site-quote precondition REMOVED 2026-09-17 by q2_colo_recut_ruling): the wholesale_batch_floor tiers put to at least one provider AFTER wholesale-service-boundary has selected a boundary for that buyer, carried to a stated price, volume, term and reliability requirement or to an explicit no. WHY THE SITE QUOTE IS NO LONGER A PRECONDITION, and why this item did NOT follow the rack to Q2 instead (review 67304, finding 2, correctly caught that the edge was cut while this sentence still demanded the quote). Restoring the edge was tried and refused by arithmetic: colo-shortlist is upstream of this item, which is upstream of the year-end gate, so a rack dependency here propagates through dep_milestone_order_ok to SIXTY-EIGHT items - s1-gate, boot-minimum, dogfood-started and the LLC filing among them - which would say the year-end gate is a Q2 object. It is not. So the precondition is removed rather than honoured: THE PRICE BASIS FOR A YEAR-END OFFER IS THE RESIDENCE FLOOR (strategy.home_hosting and strategy.cost_floor, reached through fleet-capacity-floor, which this item already depends on), NOT a colo site quote. WHAT THIS ITEM MAY THEREFORE NOT DO: quote a rack-based price, or state a volume the residence cannot energise - home_hosting admits roughly eight nodes, not twenty - and a colo-based price is a Q2 re-quote against colo-shortlist once that lands. An explicit no remains a valid exit, and is the likelier one at residence volumes. The pre-split text follows as provenance. A scoped conversation with at least one CI provider that resells arm64 capacity - Ubicloud, Blacksmith or a peer - about supplying dedicated Ampere capacity underneath their product, carried to a stated price, volume and reliability requirement or to an explicit no. THE ARITHMETIC THAT MAKES THIS WORTH DOING, and it is not the one the idea starts from: selling supply means competing with HETZNER, not with the reseller. THE 56.72 DOLLAR FIGURE THAT USED TO STAND HERE IS A RECORDED ERROR: it was hourly 0.0777 times 730, and the vendor's monthly cap on the dollar console is 48.49 (extdeps.cloud.hetzner cax41_listed), which strategy.node_rental now reads. strategy.cost_floor puts our priced floor at 67 at full utilisation, 112 at sixty percent and 225 at the selected thirty. Read naively against 48.49 our cost is still not below Hetzner RETAIL at the planning utilisation. THAT COMPARISON IS ALSO NOT A FLOOR IN THIS CORPUS: admissible_as_competitive_floor is false for every market row because our EVIDENCE is incomplete, which is not the same as the market having no floor. Three things cut the other way and they are why it does not. FIRST, THE COMPARISON IS NOT LIKE-FOR-LIKE IN OUR FAVOUR: hetzner_row records CAX41 as SharedBurstable and cites the vendor's own recommendation of dedicated instances for sustained CPU-heavy work, which is exactly what CI is; our unit is a dedicated physical core, so the comparable Hetzner product is their dedicated line and not this one. SECOND, AND THIS IS THE REAL ARGUMENT: the thirty percent utilisation figure is a DIRECT-SALES assumption, because bursty developer demand is what makes it thirty. A supply contract is bulk, steady and predictable, which is the only realistic route to the utilisation at which our floor is competitive at all - so wholesale does not merely tolerate the denominator, it is the thing that fixes it. THIRD, most of the eight unquoted components in cost_floor are per-end-user costs - support, abuse response, payment processing - and one contract with one invoice does not carry them, so the wholesale floor sits closer to its priced subtotal than the direct floor does. A FOURTH ARGUMENT FOR, ADDED 2026-09-10, AND IT IS THE ONE THIS ITEM WAS MISSING -- SUPPLY, NOT PRICE. The paragraph below concludes that interchangeable capacity competes on price and reliability alone with no path to differentiation. That conclusion is now contradicted by our own measurement and is left standing beside it rather than quietly edited, because the correction is the point. strategy.node_rental hetzner_row carries supply derived from three dated not-orderable displays to this account, not SupplyUnobserved. The three account-console readings remain this-account observations and not order attempts. Request 2026091103000909 adds a different, dated vendor statement: on 2026-09-11 Hetzner support attributed the CAX Cloud unavailability to capacity rather than an account requirement and said no additional CAX capacity was offered for new deployments, with no provisioning date or reservation. That answer is scoped to CAX Cloud and is dynamic; it does not establish dedicated Ampere availability, a quantity/price/term quote, setup fees, volume pricing, or resale conditions. Dedicated Ampere remains unanswered and cannot be cited from the CAX reply. For the wholesale direction, the exact implication is that currently unpurchasable CAX cannot stand as an available retail cost floor; it is not a market-wide Arm scarcity claim. AND THE US HALF IS UNESTABLISHED IN EITHER DIRECTION: hetzner_row is now RegionObserved for Helsinki (hel1) and Nuremberg (nbg1); Ashburn and the rest of North America were not in that reading. US-located dedicated Ampere capacity may well be the sharper differentiator than either price or aggregate supply, and this corpus currently cannot say so -- establishing it is a prerequisite to using it. AGAINST, and none of these are fatal but all must be answered before a price is quoted: Hetzner's retail contains their margin so their cost is below the listed monthly and we would be selling near ours against someone selling above theirs; one wholesale buyer is concentration risk who can dictate terms and leave; a reseller will require reliability evidence this fleet cannot yet produce, since the infrastructure pair has no failover exercise and the residence has no site-admission receipt; and interchangeable capacity competes on price and reliability alone with no path to differentiation. OWNERSHIP: EconomicsQa owns the census (wholesale-argument-grounding). AcquisitionMarketing owns this conversation. THE CONCLUSION IS BOTH CHANNELS AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL: direct sales carry margin and prove the product but cannot fill the fleet; wholesale fills the fleet and fixes utilisation but carries little margin. Each one's failure mode is the other's strength, which is what makes them complementary rather than a choice. A CONSEQUENCE FOR THE MODEL, filed here because it is where it was found: strategy.ci_site_cost selected_utilization_permille is ONE number serving both channels, and two channels with structurally different load shapes cannot share a utilisation constant - it has to become a per-channel fact or every per-unit cost derived from it is an average of two things nobody sells.",
  depends_on: [dep(id: "wholesale-service-boundary"), dep(id: "fleet-capacity-floor"), dep(id: "wholesale-argument-grounding")],
  relates_to: ["strategy.cost_floor", "strategy.node_rental", "strategy.wholesale_buyer", "strategy.wholesale_batch_floor", "strategy.ci_site_cost", "strategy.ci_runner_economics"],
  item_relations: no_item_relations,
  status: NotStarted,
}

