// THE REVERSAL COST OF THE SITING DECISION, AS A CARRIER RATHER THAN A SENTENCE (review 67321).
// siting-decision's exit asks for two things: the fork resolved, AND the reversal cost named -- a
// soft launch that cannot migrate to colo without a customer-visible outage is a different
// commitment from one that can. The ruling answered the first. Its Done note then FORWARDED the
// second to wholesale-apartment-journey-acceptance, which does not carry it: that item is a boot,
// handoff, fault and offboard journey on the apartment overlay, with no migration rehearsal and no
// outage measurement anywhere in it. A promise pointed at a row that does not hold it is worse
// than an unnamed obligation, because it reads as tracked. It is tracked here instead, and
// siting-decision cites this id.
//
// IT DOES NOT NEED THE COLO TO BE ANSWERED, which is the whole reason it can sit at year end
// rather than following the rack to Q2. The question is whether a SERVED customer can be moved
// between sites we control without a visible interruption, and the discriminating rehearsal is
// residence-host to any second host we hold -- the colo is one destination, not the property under
// test. Deferring this to Q2 would answer it after year-end customers already depend on the
// residence, which is exactly when the answer stops being able to change anything.
data item_residence_migration_rehearsal: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "residence-migration-rehearsal",
  workstream: OpsMachineHealth,
  milestone: M3YearEndGate,
  rank: 30,
  min_calendar_days: 5,
  active_effort_days: 3,
  execution_mode: AgentParallel,
  exit_criterion: "ONE SERVED WORKLOAD MOVED OFF ITS RESIDENCE HOST TO A SECOND HOST WE CONTROL, WITH THE CUSTOMER-VISIBLE EFFECT MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED: what the consumer observed during the move - requests refused, requests delayed and by how much, work lost and re-executed, or nothing at all - read from the consumer's side, not from ours. A migration nobody was consuming during measures the mechanism and not the cost, so the workload must be under load. THE OUTPUT IS A NUMBER AND A CLASS, not a pass: either the move is invisible to the consumer, or it costs a bounded interruption whose size is stated, or it cannot be done without a visible outage - and the third is a real answer that changes what may be promised to a year-end customer, not a failure of this item. WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT REQUIRE: a colocation facility. The property under test is whether a served customer can move between sites at all; the rack is one destination and is a Q2 object.",
  depends_on: [dep(id: "home-soft-launch-readiness")],
  relates_to: ["strategy.home_hosting", "strategy.fleet_convergence", "strategy.wholesale_journeys"],
  item_relations: no_item_relations,
  status: NotStarted,
}

data item_siting_decision: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "siting-decision",
  workstream: Hardware,
  milestone: M3YearEndGate,
  rank: 40,
  min_calendar_days: 3,
  active_effort_days: 3,
  execution_mode: OperatorExclusive,
  exit_criterion: "A DATED FORK, resolved: serve from colo, or soft-launch from the residence with colo as the scaling path. RESOLVED 2026-09-17 on the second arm by q2_colo_recut_ruling. BOTH DEPENDENCIES ARE CUT RATHER THAN WAITED OUT, because the operator decided this fork WITHOUT either of them -- no vendor comparison and no residence-readiness evidence -- and a dependency the decision did not consume would misdescribe what grounded it. That is a statement of RISK, not of confidence: the decision is made, and the facts that would have qualified it are still outstanding under home-soft-launch-readiness. WHAT THE RULING STATES: residence soft-launch, approximately five machines or fewer, two to three customers, colo as the Q2 scaling path. WHAT IT DOES NOT STATE, and what this item therefore does not claim: the REVERSAL COST. The original exit asks for it -- a soft launch that cannot migrate to colo without a customer-visible outage is a different commitment from one that can -- and no migration rehearsal has been run, so that half is an open obligation carried by RESIDENCE-MIGRATION-REHEARSAL rather than a fact this row establishes.",
  depends_on: no_deps,
  relates_to: ["strategy.ci_site_fleet", "strategy.home_hosting", "strategy.colo_market"],
  item_relations: no_item_relations,
  status: Done {
    evidence: [CorpusDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "strategy.year_end_plan", decl_name: "q2_colo_recut_ruling") }],
    note: "The fork is answered; the residence is the serving site through year end and the rack is a Q2 deployment. This Done covers the DECISION and nothing downstream of it: home-soft-launch-readiness still has to be established, and the reversal cost named in the exit is unmeasured. THAT OBLIGATION HAS A CARRIER: residence-migration-rehearsal, added in the same change (review 67321). An earlier cut of this note forwarded it to wholesale-apartment-journey-acceptance, which does not hold it -- that item is a boot, handoff, fault and offboard journey with no migration rehearsal and no outage measurement in it - so the obligation read as tracked while being untracked.",
  },
}

data item_netboot_gap_analysis: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "netboot-gap-analysis",
  workstream: Hardware,
  milestone: M1bInternalS0Proof,
  rank: 20,
  min_calendar_days: 5,
  active_effort_days: 5,
  execution_mode: AgentParallel,
  exit_criterion: "END-TO-END netboot install enumerated against what actually exists: DHCP/PXE or HTTP boot path, aarch64 UEFI chain, image identity and digest, per-node identity and secret injection, disk-free or disk-optional operation, unattended reinstall, and recovery when the boot service is the thing that is down. Each step is Present, Partial or Absent with evidence — an Absent that nobody wrote down is the one that surfaces during the first reinstall of a customer-serving node. THE HOST BOOT VARIANT IS ALREADY SETTLED AND THIS ITEM MUST NOT REOPEN IT: strategy.compute_fabric_model selects RamRoot with local NVMe as disposable scratch only, netboot-m0 states RAM-root, and the reasoning is recorded - a root materialised into RAM limits the network dependency to the BOOT WINDOW, where an NFS root keeps the network on a running node's continuous execution path so losing the server hangs a live node. What is open is the layer BELOW the host root: guest and scratch materialisation, and whether a node-local cache exists at all, which compute_fabric_model already carries as the choice between NetworkToRamNoNodeCache, NetworkToRamWithSiteCache and NetworkToLocalEphemeralRoot. Its diskless_promotion_gate owes eight controls before local scratch may be removed, and control six - that removing local scratch adds the EXACT remote-scratch network demand - is the one this fleet's 1 GbE links make expensive to get wrong. Precedes netboot-m0, which builds the product this analysis scopes.",
  depends_on: [dep(id: "hw-first-host")],
  relates_to: ["strategy.mobo_x_architecture", "strategy.compute_fabric_model"],
  item_relations: [CitesPeerStatement { of: "netboot-m0", why: "netboot-m0 already states RAM-root; this analysis must not reopen the host-root variant. The same sentence also says this item precedes netboot-m0, which WOULD be blocking and belongs as dep() on netboot-m0 if anyone establishes that order - it is not this carrier." }],
  status: NotStarted,
}

data item_node_burnin_protocol: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "node-burnin-protocol",
  workstream: OpsMachineHealth,
  milestone: M1bInternalS0Proof,
